---
// Privacy policy for the open source first site. It discloses the CLI's Sentry crash reports (on by default, see cli/src/lib/sentry.ts) and Full's
// opt-in error reporting (wordpress-plugin/src/Sentry/Consent.php), and Full's opt-out usage ping
// (wordpress-plugin/src/Update/Infrastructure/UsagePing.php). The console section stays as
// long as the console runs, even though the site no longer promotes it.
import DocPage from "@/components/site/DocPage.astro";

export const prerender = true;

const LAST_UPDATED = "November 24, 2026";
---

<DocPage title="Loopress Privacy Policy: What Data Reaches Us" description="Privacy policy of Loopress: it runs on your machine and your sites. Here is the little that reaches us, and how to turn it off." kicker={`privacy · last updated ${LAST_UPDATED}`} heading="Privacy." lede="Loopress runs on your machine and your sites. Here is the little that reaches us, and how to turn it off.">
  <h2>The CLI</h2>
  <p>
    <code>lps</code> talks to your WordPress sites directly. Your files, credentials and site data
    never pass through Loopress servers.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the CLI crashes, it sends an error report to Sentry so we can fix it. Before it leaves your
    machine, the report is scrubbed: arguments and flag values are removed (only flag names are
    kept), site URLs are blanked, and server responses are dropped. Turn it off with
    <code>lps telemetry disable</code>, or for a single run with <code>LOOPRESS_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The WordPress plugin</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light</strong> sends nothing to Loopress.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Full</strong> can report its own errors to Sentry, only after an administrator says yes in
      the plugin's Settings tab. It also checks for new versions, like any plugin update check.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Full</strong> also sends an anonymous usage ping, at most twice a day, so we know how many
      sites use it: a random identifier generated by the plugin, the Loopress, WordPress and PHP
      versions, the site language, its timezone and its environment type (production, staging...). Never
      the site URL, an email address, content or the plugin list, and the IP address is not stored.
      Daily rows are deleted after 13 months. Switch it off anytime in the plugin's Settings tab.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contact form</strong>: your name, email and message, used only to reply.</li>
    <li><strong>Newsletter</strong>: if you subscribe, your email is stored with Brevo. Every email has an unsubscribe link.</li>
    <li><strong>Analytics</strong>: Vercel Analytics, aggregated and without cookies.</li>
    <li><strong>Theme</strong>: one local storage entry remembers light or dark mode. No tracking or advertising cookies.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Logging in with the CLI</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing is sent from the CLI to the console until you log in. Logging in, with <code>lps login</code>
    or by setting <code>LOOPRESS_TOKEN</code>, is your consent to send two kinds of report, and it is
    the legal basis for processing them (consent, GDPR article 6(1)(a)):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Deployment records</strong>, after each real push: the target URL, the status (success or
      failure), the command that ran, and, when the project is a Git repository, the commit hash, its
      subject line and whether the working tree had uncommitted changes. A full <code>lps push</code>
      also lists its steps with their status. After a plugin push, the plugins listed in
      <code>loopress.json</code> (slug, pinned version, whether active).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Drift reports</strong>, after <code>lps diff</code>: the target URL, the verdict, the exit
      code, and for each difference the resource, the id and the kind of change. Never file contents or
      patches. <code>lps plugin audit</code> sends its advisories the same way: plugin slug, version,
      CVE, severity, fixed version, title and health notes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    No credential (Application Password, token) and no content (posts, option values, file contents) is
    part of these reports. To stop, run <code>lps logout</code> or unset <code>LOOPRESS_TOKEN</code>:
    nothing is sent afterwards, and nothing else changes. Withdrawing consent does not affect what was
    sent before; ask us to delete it through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The Loopress console</h2>
  <p>
    If you sign in to console.loopress.dev with Google, we store your email address and a Google
    account identifier, never your Google password. Running <code>lps login</code> connects a project
    to that account and syncs its metadata and WordPress credentials, encrypted at rest.
  </p>

  <h2>Who processes it</h2>
  <p>
    Usage pings are stored on Loopress's own servers, never shared. Sentry (error reports), Brevo (email), Vercel (hosting and analytics), Google (console sign-in)
    and GitHub (code and releases), each under its own privacy policy. We do not sell your data or
    share it for anyone's marketing.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask to access, correct, export or delete your data at any time through the
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. We keep it while your account is active or as long as
    needed to answer you.
  </p>

  <p class="aside">loopress is a developer tool, not aimed at children under 16.</p>
</DocPage>
